  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: 'The Election Commission of India (ECI) is an autonomous constitutional
    authority responsible for administering elections in India. It was established
    on 25 January 1950, and its powers and responsibilities are mainly provided under
    Article 324 of the Constitution of India. The Constitution gives the Election
    Commission the responsibility for the superintendence, direction, and control
    of electoral rolls and elections to Parliament, State Legislatures, and the offices
    of the President and Vice-President of India. Legislative.gov.in

    The Election
    Commission manages different stages of the election process. It plans election
    schedules, supervises electoral-roll preparation, determines polling arrangements,
    and oversees the conduct of polling and counting. Election authorities also arrange
    polling stations and assign voters to polling stations so that voting can be
    conducted systematically across the country. Election Commission of India

    During
    elections, officials and polling personnel work under the election machinery
    to manage polling stations and carry out election-related duties. The Election
    Commission also issues instructions and guidelines for conducting elections and
    oversees important aspects of the process through election officials and observers.
    Election Commission of India

    After polling is completed, votes are counted
    according to the prescribed electoral process and the results are declared. Through
    these responsibilities, the Election Commission oversees the election process
    from planning and preparation through polling, counting, and results. Election
    Commission of India'
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: ecf77945acdc26144b2f70e021c9fd31, type: 2}
